# rwv add

## Purpose

Clone a repo and register it in the active project's `rwv.toml`, then
re-run integration activation so ecosystem files (Cargo workspace, npm
workspace, etc.) are updated to include the new member.

`add` is an **intent verb**: it mutates the manifest and regenerates
integration-managed content so the resulting files can be committed alongside
the `rwv.toml` change.

### Clone placement

Clones always land at the **canonical path** under the primary weave root,
regardless of whether `rwv add` is invoked from inside a workweave. This
reflects the shared-clone topology: member repos are global infrastructure,
with `git worktree` providing per-workweave isolation. (See
`rwv explain workweave` for the workweave topology model.)

If a clone already exists on disk at the canonical path and its origin names
the same repository, `add` skips the network clone and registers it. A clone
of anything else refuses (`foreign-clone-at-placement`), naming `rwv add
--adopt <path>` to register it under its own origin.

### Workweave-aware worktree materialization

When `rwv add` is invoked from inside a workweave, it also creates a
`git worktree` for the new repo inside the workweave directory. The
ephemeral branch follows the standard `{project}--{workweave}` naming
convention (matching `rwv workweave create`) — flat, with no third component
derived from the source branch. `add` records an ownership receipt for it, so
`rwv workweave delete` destroys it along with the rest of the workweave's
refs. If the canonical clone has no commits yet (from `--new`), the worktree
creation is skipped; run `rwv sync` after the first commit to materialize it.

### Adopting an existing clone (`--adopt`)

With `--adopt`, the argument is the path of a clone already under the primary
weave root, and that path is the one recorded. `add` reads the URL from the
clone's `origin` remote and registers it without a network clone. A path that
holds no clone refuses (`no-clone-to-adopt`).

### Spelling the argument

The argument's meaning comes from how it is spelled. A URL — `https://`,
`git@`, or `file://` for a local remote — or registry shorthand such as
`github/owner/repo` is cloned from. An existing clone is registered only under `--adopt`.
Anything else reads as a path, which could mean either, so `add` refuses
(`unresolvable-repo-source`) and names both spellings: `rwv add
file://<absolute path>` to clone from a local remote, and `rwv add --adopt
<path>` to register the clone at that path.

### Creation (`--new`)

`--new` is expected to be rare. The common path is to create the repo with
the registry's own tools (a forge's web UI, `gh`/`glab`, or `mkdir` + `git
init` for a local one) and then run a plain `rwv add <url>`, which already
works for anything `placement` covers. `--new` is a secondary convenience for
when that isn't done yet.

With `--new`, the positional argument is a **creation address**, not a URL or
a path — a bare registry name (`local`), or a three-segment shorthand
(`github/acme/fresh`) that fills the `owner` and `repo` parameters for
whichever registry the first segment names. Any parameter the registry still
needs — every parameter, for a bare registry name — is supplied with
`--param <name>=<value>` (repeatable) or as one JSON object via
`--params-json '{"name": "value"}'`. A parameter named in more than one of
these three places refuses rather than picking a winner.

Creation is registry-dispatched: each registry declares its own parameters
and plans what gets created from them.

{{MSG:creation_params_table}}

- **A domain registry** (`github`, `gitlab`, `bitbucket`) needs `owner` and
  `repo`. Nothing is created remotely — rwv has no HTTP client — so the
  member is `git init`'d locally and its remote is set to the minted URL as
  an intent. `rwv add github/acme/fresh --new` and `rwv add github --new
  --param owner=acme --param repo=fresh` are equivalent.
- **`local`** needs `root`, `owner`, and `repo`. Creation is two-sided: rwv
  creates a **bare** repository at `<root>/<owner>/<repo>` and clones the
  member from it, so the member's remote is real from birth and `git push
  -u` works immediately. `root` must already exist (rwv creates
  `<root>/<owner>/<repo>`, not `<root>` itself) and must be outside the
  weave. `rwv add local/acme/fresh --new` fills `owner` and `repo` from the
  shorthand and still needs `--param root=<dir>`.

The placement path is always derived from the minted URL, the same way a
plain `rwv add <url>` derives it — the creation address only selects a
registry and supplies parameters, never a layout.

## Invocation

```
rwv add <url> [--role <role>] [--project <name>]
rwv add <path> --adopt [--role <role>] [--project <name>]
rwv add <creation-address> --new [--param <name>=<value>]... [--params-json <json>] [--role <role>] [--project <name>]
```

- `<url>` — repository URL or shorthand. Parsed through the registry
  topology to derive the canonical local path. Accepts `https://`, `git@`,
  `file://`, or registry shorthand such as `github/owner/repo`.
- `--adopt` — the argument is the path of an existing clone under the primary
  weave root, registered without cloning (see "Adopting an existing clone").
- `--role <role>` — role for the repo in the manifest (`owned`, `fork`,
  `dependency`, `reference`). Defaults to `owned`.
- `--new` — create a new repo instead of cloning. The positional argument
  becomes a creation address (see "Creation" above) rather than a URL or path.
- `--param <name>=<value>` — a creation parameter (repeatable). See the table
  above for what each registry declares.
- `--params-json <json>` — the same parameters as one JSON object of string
  values.
- `--project <name>` — operate on this project rather than the active project.
  Does not change `.rwv-active`.

Run `rwv --help add` for the full clap surface.

## Output

Progress messages to stderr:

- `Added '<repo-path>' to manifest` — repo was registered.
- `Repository already exists in manifest at '<repo-path>'; re-running its
  activation` — the manifest is left as it is, and activation runs again, so a
  weave an earlier run left short (a surfaced link gone missing, install hooks
  that failed) is repaired by repeating the same command. An entry written into
  `rwv.toml` by hand is finished the same way: when its canonical clone is not
  on disk, `add` clones it from the URL the manifest records, creates the
  workweave's worktree when run in one, then regenerates. The URL given must
  name the same repo as the manifest's entry, or the add refuses
  (`occupied-placement`).
- `Directory already exists at '<path>', skipping clone` — clone present,
  not re-cloned.

Integration activation output (symlinks created/removed) goes to stderr.

## Exit codes

- `0` — repo added (or already present), manifest updated, activation
  succeeded.
- non-zero — URL could not be resolved, clone failed, manifest could not be
  written, or activation returned an error.

The manifest edit and the regeneration it drives land together or not at all.
When activation refuses before the install hooks run — an integration rejects
the new member, or a surfacing path is occupied — `rwv.toml` and every
regenerated file are put back as they were, and the error says so. The
install hooks run after that point and have no undo, so when one fails the
error says `rwv.toml` was updated; re-running the add re-runs them. A failed
hook is not a refused add: the member stands.

A refused add accounts for what it had created by then. A clone it fetched,
and a workweave worktree it made, stay on disk: the error names each one,
says that re-running the same add after fixing the cause reuses it, and names
`rwv remove <path> --delete` as the way to remove it instead (in the
workweave for its worktree, in the primary weave for the clone). What
`--new` created — the bare upstream under `--param root`, the new member, and
any directory made to hold them — holds nothing but what the add put there,
so the refused add removes it and says so.

## Examples

Add an owned repo (clone + register):

```
rwv add https://github.com/myorg/myrepo
```

Add a dependency (read-only; won't be pushed or edited):

```
rwv add https://github.com/third-party/lib --role dependency
```

Register a repo that's already cloned:

```
rwv add --adopt github/myorg/existing-repo
```

Clone from a local bare repository:

```
rwv add file:///srv/repos/myorg/lib.git
```

Create a repo at a registry that hosts it (member `git init`'d locally,
remote set to the minted URL as an intent):

```
rwv add github/myorg/new-project --new
```

Create a repo with no registry backing it, from scratch — rwv creates a bare
upstream under `--param root` and clones the member from it:

```
rwv add local --new --param root=/srv/repos --param owner=myorg --param repo=new-project
```

The same creation, scripted:

```
rwv add local --new --params-json '{"root": "/srv/repos", "owner": "myorg", "repo": "new-project"}'
```

## Common errors

- *unrecognized URL* — the URL does not parse through any built-in registry
  and has no derivable local path. Check the URL format.
- *not a URL and no registry reads it as shorthand*
  (`unresolvable-repo-source`) — the argument reads as a path. Spell a local remote as `file://<absolute path>`, or pass
  `--adopt` to register the clone at that path.
- *no clone to adopt* (`no-clone-to-adopt`) — `--adopt` names a path with no
  clone.
- *foreign clone at placement* (`foreign-clone-at-placement`) — the URL's
  placement already holds a clone of a different repository; register it with
  `--adopt`, or move it aside.
- *clone failed* — network error or authentication problem; inspect git output.
- *manifest parse failure* — `rwv.toml` could not be loaded; verify the file
  is valid TOML.
- *missing creation parameter* — `--new` and a required parameter (see the
  table above) was not supplied by the address, `--param`, or `--params-json`.
- *duplicate creation parameter* — the same parameter name arrived through
  more than one of the address shorthand, `--param`, and `--params-json`.
- *occupied placement* — the URL's or creation address's placement already
  maps to a different URL in the manifest.
